Realtime volumetric cloud work
=============================

Authority and scope
  Source: user-requested cloud performance analysis and follow-up tasklist request (2026-10-18).
  Parent: docs/PBR.BaselineShading.todo, physically motivated volumetric clouds.
  Inherited contracts: docs/PBR.VolumetricClouds.Proposal.md, archived hierarchy proposal,
    docs/PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md and the existing native six-attachment OIT contract.
  Related checklists: docs/PBR.VolumetricClouds.todo and archived hierarchy checklist.
  Hierarchy archive: commit f66895c6, docs/PBR.CloudHierarchy.Proposal.md and
    docs/PBR.CloudHierarchy.todo (read with git show <commit>:<path>). These removed documents
    supply inherited geometry/ownership contracts, not current architecture or open task authority.
  Paths prefixed docs/, artifacts/ or VanillaGraphicsExpanded/ are repository-root references;
    sibling Markdown links resolve from the containing document's directory.
  Phase 1 records the measured baseline and original quality policy without changing production rendering.
  Its approximation thresholds and the historical Phase 2 receipts below are superseded by the
  current correctness-only test policy; they are retained as historical measurements.
  Runtime implementation belongs to the subsequent increments; completion requires their own evidence.
  Use bounded ray budgets, reduced tracing, practical termination and amortized transport
    while retaining VGE's native 2D column envelope and procedural density detail.
  This follow-up intentionally revisits the inherited fixed half-resolution default and preservation
    of per-column sample counts/PMJ indices. Reconcile those clauses explicitly before implementation;
    do not misrepresent changed quadrature as exact equivalence to the earlier renderer.
  Preserve native weather, map units/validity, cubic bounds, connected faces, wind anchoring,
    solar/lunar physical inputs, one approximate scattering octave, scene encoding and OIT ordering.
  Retain same-frame native publication, shared material depth plus primary/liquid clipping,
    change-driven enablement, Off/Simple behavior and resource lifetime ownership in both PBR modes.
  Do not add a complete density volume, replacement weather producer, installed shader edits,
    CPU map readback, generic rendering framework or reflection consumer. The existing reflected-cloud
    integration dependency remains tracked by the parent, not silently satisfied by this work.
  Reduced tracing uses same-frame internal attenuation for the stronger source by default, following
    explicit user authorization after the Phase 8 experiment. Bounded secondary marching remains
    the other-source, reference and fallback path. This does not authorize cast shadows onto scene/atmosphere consumers, an earlier
    native producer or previous-frame shadow publication; those shadows remain deferred to virtual
    shadow maps. Preserve current saved settings/defaults while removing repeated implementation work.

Current test policy (user direction, 2026-10-18)
  Automated tests must not enforce approximation quality. Existing cloud approximation-quality
    assertions are removed by Phase 3.1; historical exclusions are not current verification filters.
    This supersedes approximation-error thresholds and baseline-quality requirements in this file
    and inherited documents, including previously recorded acceptance policies. Historical receipts
    remain evidence of what was measured, not current requirements or justification for sample counts.
  Keep correctness tests for the specified finite-budget algorithm: sample positions/counts/weights,
    physical units, shared density, valid domain/depth clipping, finite bounded outputs, settings,
    resource ownership, publication, history admission and exact OIT/encoding arithmetic.
    Floating-point tolerances for those mathematical contracts remain legitimate.
  Comparisons with denser/exact transport, other sample budgets, reconstructed reference images or
    frozen noise/RMS baselines may report diagnostics, but must not assert acceptable approximation
    error, convergence, noise reduction or visual fidelity. This applies to primary/secondary tracing,
    termination, reconstruction and atmospheric approximations, including existing passing tests.
  Current verification requires no approximation-test exclusions. Mixed tests retain their independent
    correctness coverage; historical excluded cases were never counted as passing tests.
    Do not raise sample counts to satisfy retired quality gates. Runtime defaults remain unchanged
    by this policy edit; performance and user-run appearance acceptance remain separate work.

Execution and evidence
  Keep domain-focused files, thin composition roots, directional dependencies, documented methods
    and classes, visibility regions and comments explaining nontrivial logic.
  Use existing GPU owners, program/binding contracts, submission, shader registration and cleanup.
  Run build/test tools through subagents. Routine correctness stays lightweight; full-size captures
    and timing are explicit qualification. Never launch the game for visual acceptance.
  Separate source findings, independent numerical checks, GPU correctness, packaging, controlled
    GPU timing and user-run live acceptance. Leave required acceptance gates open without evidence.
  Reconcile linked documents, perform a second review and an independent completion audit before
    marking each increment complete. Preserve correctness assertions; remove approximation-quality
    acceptance assertions under the current test policy rather than widening their tolerances.

Technical direction
  Budget samples across complete clipped rays, with explicit caps and distance scaling.
  Bound secondary tracing distance independently and concentrate shadow samples near their origin.
  Evaluate quarter-dimension tracing, half-dimension reconstruction and full-view upsampling.
  Qualify practical transmittance termination against all required physical and OIT outputs.
  Evaluate fixed phase terms once per primary ray/light and amortize atmospheric composition.
  Reject proven empty support before expensive density evaluation. Select acceleration by measured
    total benefit; retaining a min/max tree is not itself an acceptance requirement.
  Select counts and distances for Vintage Story's world scale and native finite grid.

Phase 1: Freeze baseline and specify bounded quality policy
  Outcome: the implementation has explicit work limits, comparison modes and numerical criteria.
  ☑ Reverify current VGE source and the technical direction, recording source identities and settings.
    - Trace CloudVolumeSettings/CloudRenderingSettings, CloudVolumeParameters, CloudIntegrate,
      CloudLightDepth, CloudHierarchySeek, reconstruction, history and atmospheric/OIT composition.
    - Treat artifacts/CloudHierarchy/phase6-512-result-summary.json as historical controlled evidence;
      establish a fresh candidate rather than assigning its timings to changed current sources.
  ☑ Freeze a current baseline and record hardware, resolution, settings, shader selection and hashes.
    - Measure view/secondary work, hierarchy construction, trace, temporal, resolve, CPU submission
      and total owned cost. Diagnostic counters must be opt-in and excluded from final timed binaries.
    - Reuse the benchmark scheduler; retain DDA/hierarchy matched comparisons and raw observations.
  ☑ Define explicit whole-ray view/light budgets, distance scaling, short-ray minimums, finite caps,
    independent shadow distance, termination error limits and reduced-resolution quality selections.
    - Record world/metre conversion, thin-feature sampling limits, primary jitter progression and
      coverage of the complete clipped span. Do not truncate the ray when its budget is exhausted.
    - Define independent high-budget/reference modes and quantitative radiance, attenuation, all
      OIT-channel, depth and temporal acceptance limits before tuning against candidate outputs.
    - Use a clean break for replaced settings: obsolete per-column fields cannot silently become
      whole-ray counts of four/one. Missing current fields use defaults; validate invalid settings.
    - Use a .005 threshold and quarter-dimension trace as starting candidates, not established
      safe defaults. Select actual budgets/distances from VGE scene scale and measured quality.
  ☑ Reconcile the inherited proposal/checklist clauses being revised and map every finding below
    to its implementation/verification task. Preserve unaffected ownership and physical contracts.
  ☑ Completion: a reproducible baseline, bounded policy, settings rules and numerical acceptance
    criteria exist; neither prior timings nor new policy choices are reported as delivered behavior.

Phase 2: Bound primary integration independently of native grid crossings
  Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: view-density/shading samples obey a complete-ray budget.
  ☑ Replace per-occupied-column subdivision allocation in cloud_volume_transport.glsl with the
    bounded distance-scaled primary policy; distinguish geometry traversal from shading samples.
    - Clip the supported ray before budgeting; support camera-inside, thin/short intervals, grazing
      rays, near foreground clips and finite-map exits. Never extend native border columns.
    - Use uniform distance steps across the complete clipped span, including empty gaps, with one
      spatial blue-noise offset per primary ray. Preserve segment lengths and extinction units.
      Wire the noise asset through dedicated cloud program/binding and resource-lifetime contracts;
      remove obsolete cloud PMJ bindings when the last legacy consumer is retired.
      Secondary PMJ use may remain temporarily until Phase 3 replaces that integration path.
    - Evaluate the actual shared density at each scheduled position. Use cheap native support checks
      to reject empty samples, without redistributing them to occupied intervals or adding samples.
      Narrow features can fall between samples; measure that finite-budget limitation explicitly.
    - Keep the sample lattice independent of hierarchy visits. Retain exact optical depth for uniform
      density fixtures; arbitrary discontinuous columns use an independent analytic reference for error
      measurement, not a requirement for exact full-support integration at every production budget.
  ☑ Verify sample-count bounds and independent interval/transport results on empty/sparse/dense maps,
    odd dimensions, separated heights, tiny positive extinction, boundary ties and detail on/off.
    - Preserve finite-lattice arithmetic and exact same-algorithm equivalence. Comparisons against
      a frozen baseline or higher-budget oracle are optional diagnostics without quality thresholds.
  ☑ Completion: occupied-cell count no longer multiplies primary shading samples without bound;
    finite-domain, complete-span uniform-density and algorithm checks pass in both PBR selections.

Phase 3: Bound secondary solar/lunar integration
  Depends on Phase 2. Outcome: every contributing light has an independent whole-ray work budget.
  ☑ Replace per-column secondary sample allocation in cloud_volume_light.glsl with bounded counts
    and a separate physical tracing distance, with early exit at the actual supported cloud domain.
    - Use quadratic distance interval boundaries and their arithmetic midpoints as shadow positions,
      with density weighted by each interval's physical length. Do not square the midpoint index,
      jitter shadows or move their samples according to occupancy/optical depth.
    - Budget the complete bounded shadow span, not one complete budget per visited column.
      Preserve off-camera self-lighting; main-view scene-depth clips must not clip secondary rays.
    - Skip zero contributing sources before secondary setup. Assess any negligible-light cutoff
      against admitted physical radiance bounds rather than a hardcoded daylight assumption.
    - Expose bounded settings through existing configuration/binding contracts and history
      invalidation. Replace LightSamples with the new fields using a clean break and defaults;
      do not convert saved per-column counts. Shadow-map replacement is outside this increment;
      the later Phase 8 evaluation does not retroactively qualify a replacement here.
  ☑ Verify optical-depth integration and illumination against independent/high-budget references,
    including low-angle sun/moon, inside-cloud origins, distant occluders and shadow-distance limits.
    - Quantify bias from finite shadow distance and coarse samples separately from hierarchy errors.
      Cover solar-only, lunar-only, both lights, zero light and all multiple-scattering coefficients.
    - Assert the specified finite-budget integration and source/OIT arithmetic. Differences from
      exact or higher-budget transport are diagnostics only, with no approximation-quality gate.
  ☑ Completion: shadow sample counts/distance are bounded independently of occupied column count;
    integration and additional-octave correctness pass; approximation diagnostics are not blockers.

Phase 3.1: Remove cloud approximation-quality acceptance tests
  Depends on Phase 3. Outcome: automated tests enforce implementation correctness only.
  ☑ Inventory cloud tests and shared fixtures for approximation-quality assertions, including passing
    tests. Cover CloudPrimaryOracleTests, CloudPrimaryBudgetTests, CloudSecondaryBudgetTests,
    CloudVolumeDrawTests, CloudVolumeReconstructionTests and helpers used by those tests.
    - Identify gates on radiance/attenuation/OIT discrepancy versus exact or higher-budget transport,
      reconstruction reference envelopes, sample-budget/reference-doubling stability, temporal means,
      frozen-baseline RMS and noise reduction. Include equivalent indirect or relational assertions.
    - Classify each assertion by purpose. Retain exact uniform-density Beer-Lambert fixtures only
      where the complete sampled span is actually uniform; arbitrary discontinuous support is an
      approximation comparison, even with Detail=false or a constant extinction inside each column.
  ☑ Remove approximation-quality tests/assertions. Split mixed cases to preserve meaningful algorithm,
    clipping, finite-value, physical-unit, OIT, settings, lifecycle and history-correctness coverage.
    - Do not replace removed gates with looser thresholds, golden-image fidelity checks or assertions
      that more samples must look closer to a reference. Do not leave permanently skipped quality tests.
    - Retain only useful explicitly invoked diagnostics and independent reference helpers without
      quality pass/fail assertions. Remove unused fixtures, reference-only assets and build plumbing.
    - Preserve deterministic replay and exact same-algorithm acceleration/storage equivalence checks;
      these verify implementation identity rather than acceptable approximation quality.
  ☑ Reconcile active cloud proposals/checklists/current architecture and verification filters so no
    automated approximation-quality requirement or hidden equivalent remains. Preserve raw historical
    results without presenting retired thresholds as current authority. Record remaining manual
    appearance and measured-performance work separately; do not change runtime defaults in this cleanup.
  ☑ Verify through subagents: canonical build/shader checks and focused retained correctness tests.
    Review assertion bodies and shared helpers, not only test names or numeric-literal searches.
    Complete a second review and independent completion audit before marking this sub-phase complete.
  ☑ Completion: no cloud automated test enforces approximation quality; retained correctness tests
    pass and the temporary exclusions in current verification are no longer necessary.

Phase 4: Use practical transmittance termination and qualify OIT error
  Depends on Phase 3.1. Outcome: sample-granular termination follows its configured predicate.
  ☑ Replace the fixed 1e-6 view predicate with a bounded/configured criterion evaluated at sample
    granularity, recording the .005 candidate's output differences without quality pass/fail limits.
    - Stop the primary march after a sample when overall transmittance falls below the configured
      threshold. Compare all bucket transmissions, reconstructed products, glow and support metadata
      against threshold-disabled references; do not require a separate residual-support traversal.
    - Report OIT differences for performance/appearance review. Do not add a second attenuation march
      or residual-bound subsystem to satisfy an automated approximation-quality threshold.
    - Evaluate the bounded shadow sample schedule without optical-depth early termination initially.
      Any later light-ray saturation shortcut needs separate measured benefit and quality justification,
      including zero octave occlusion/contribution endpoints. Keep finite optical-depth arithmetic.
  ☑ Verify thick/thin, bright-source, translucent overlap, particle/liquid, inside-cloud and HDR cases
    against termination-disabled references, including the first saturated sample inside a column.
  ☑ Completion: predicate placement, finite arithmetic, clipping and OIT/metadata bookkeeping pass
    correctness checks; differences from termination-disabled transport are recorded as diagnostics.

Phase 5: Trace fewer screen positions and reconstruct missing information
  Depends on Phase 4. Outcome: a quality selection traces quarter view dimensions.
  ☑ Add quarter-width/quarter-height tracing with half-dimension temporal reconstruction and
    full-resolution depth-aware texture-only resolve; retain half-dimension and explicit full-view
    reference selections for quality comparison through existing settings/resource owners.
    - Generalize CloudVolumeStorage, CloudVolumeReconstruction, CloudTemporalHistory, parameters,
      trace UVs, phase scheduling, reprojection and resolve coordinates; remove hardcoded factor-two
      assumptions where they no longer apply. Separate trace and reconstruction dimensions.
    - Quarter-dimension tracing supplies one of each 2x2 half-dimension reconstruction positions
      per frame, completing a four-frame cycle. Do not infer a sixteen-frame cycle from full-view scale.
    - Reconstruct unsampled locations from compatible history/current support; do not merely blur
      a quarter-size image or launch hidden full-resolution density/light marches at disocclusions.
    - Preserve radiance/transmission and bucket covariance rules, shared payload weights, native OIT
      products and once-only encoding. Handle odd/small extents and foreground/liquid discontinuities.
    - Reuse allocations and fixed attachments in stable frames; invalidate/recreate only for actual
      quality/layout/lifetime changes. Update nominal storage limits without claiming resident bytes.
  ☑ Verify the new phase mapping, unsampled history/current arithmetic, independent foreground clips,
    allocation reuse, resize and scale switches through existing GPU abstractions. Reuse existing
    motion/history and OIT coverage; do not add a large scene matrix that only logs differences.
    Half/full-view appearance comparisons belong to final user qualification, not mandatory tests.
  ☑ Retain Half as the shipped default and expose Quarter for diagnostic comparison. Any later
    default change requires user appearance review, including inside-cloud views and scenes where
    Half remains necessary; keep that qualification open in the final acceptance work.
  ☑ Completion: trace-position count is approximately one-sixteenth of full view in the new mode,
    reconstruction correctness passes and no fallback hides unbudgeted transport work. Approximation
    comparisons do not enforce automated quality; shipped selection still requires user appearance review.

Phase 5.1: Prune earlier cloud tests and eliminate redundant GPU work
  Depends on Phase 5. Outcome: each retained test protects a distinct correctness contract at a measured cost.
  ☑ Review the earlier test groups by asserted behavior and actual GPU work, not reported case count.
    - Cover CloudTerminationTests, CloudVolumeReconstructionTests, CloudPrimaryBudgetTests,
      CloudSecondaryBudgetTests, CloudSecondaryDensityTests and their shared fixtures.
    - Identify the concrete regression each retained case detects; consolidate overlapping coverage.
      Keep exact schedule/weight arithmetic, strict termination boundaries, independent foreground
      clips, source rejection, OIT/encoding, history and resource-lifetime contracts.
  ☑ Remove TerminationDifferencesAreDiagnosticAcrossSceneContracts from normal correctness execution.
    - Its thirteen paired scene comparisons primarily report differences and finite outputs. Retain
      focused first-saturated-step, threshold equality, disabled-exit and clipping bookkeeping tests.
      Preserve existing diagnostic receipts; further scene comparisons belong to explicit opt-in
      diagnostics or user qualification, without approximation-quality pass/fail thresholds.
  ☑ Replace SceneTransitionsKeepPayloadFiniteAndAttenuationBounded's eighteen-by-eight-frame matrix
    with focused cases only where existing history, clipping or encoding tests leave a distinct gap.
    - Require an independently authored expected response for the retained behavior. A renderer
      returning neutral OIT throughout must not pass a test intended to verify cloud transitions.
      Generic finiteness and attenuation bounds do not justify repeating every scene configuration.
  ☑ Reduce CompleteRayLatticeMatchesIndependentGeometry's Cartesian primary matrix.
    - Replace the 3,520 dispatch/readback combinations with a small explicit set covering count
      clamping, short/empty spans, finite-domain entry/exit, boundary directions, jitter and detail.
      Keep independent scalar expectations; evaluate pure scheduling arithmetic on the CPU where
      GPU execution adds no distinct coverage. Avoid repeating equivalent saturated-budget cases.
  ☑ Reduce the secondary schedule/density matrices and repeated source-field evaluation.
    - Prune AnchoredQuadraticScheduleMatchesIndependentScalarField's 2,016 dispatch/readback
      combinations while retaining anchored quadratic weights, short support, finite-domain exit,
      detail and representative budget/distance boundaries.
    - In DirectionalSourcesAndOctaveEndpointsMatchFiniteLatticeArithmetic, evaluate unchanged
      directional density inputs once rather than repeating them across source/octave combinations.
      Retain zero-source rejection, solar/lunar composition, octave endpoints and finite dense input.
    - Consolidate overlapping procedural-density and integration cases in CloudSecondaryDensityTests;
      each retained GPU probe must exercise a distinct field or integration behavior.
  ☑ Remove remaining redundant fixture work using existing GPU abstractions.
    - Load only programs needed by the assertion, request reference transport explicitly, reuse
      unchanged inputs and read each required payload once. Preserve test isolation and ownership.
      Use existing shader, texture, staging-buffer, framebuffer, command and state owners; no raw GL
      operations or new generic test framework. Keep Phase 1 measurement classes explicitly opt-in.
  ☑ Verify the retained contracts through subagents and record comparable before/after test costs.
    - Reuse applicable historical timing receipts; do not rerun the old full matrices just to obtain
      another baseline. Record actual dispatch/readback counts, per-test and aggregate durations,
      configuration and relevant regressions. Demonstrate reduced work and execution cost without
      introducing automated timing or approximation-quality acceptance thresholds.
    - Use focused affected tests with per-case progress and bounded hang diagnostics. Reconcile
      checklist references with removed/consolidated tests; retain historical receipts as history.
      Complete a second review and independent completion audit before checking off this sub-phase.
  ☑ Completion: low-value diagnostic/smoke matrices are retired or explicitly opt-in, redundant GPU
    work is removed, retained correctness contracts pass, and the reduced test cost is documented.

Phase 6: Amortize atmospheric and repeated density/phase evaluation
  Depends on Phase 5.1. Outcome: shading avoids repeated work with explicitly qualified approximations.
  ☑ Move aerial/local-fog application out of every density-shading sample toward representative-
    distance composition; derive the representative distance and apply the existing transport once.
    - Preserve physical cloud history, underwater/local-medium selection, coverage, lighting range
      admission and scene encoding. Decide the owning pass and invalidate history when required.
    - Preserve distributed OIT bucket composition: derive whether one distance is adequate or each
      bucket needs a representative distance. Do not flatten the volume to a depthless overlay.
    - Compare to existing per-sample transport for deep clouds, foreground/translucent overlap,
      horizon, underwater and changing fog; choose bounded extra distance evaluations if needed.
    - Test the specified representative-distance formula, OIT bookkeeping and once-only encoding
      independently. Differences from per-sample transport are diagnostics for measured-cost and
      user appearance review, not automated approximation-error or fidelity acceptance gates.
  ☑ Hoist fixed directional phase evaluation to once per primary ray/light and reuse it for every
    occupied sample. Check generated shader behavior so compiler hoisting is not claimed as new savings.
  ☑ Reduce repeated cloud_volume_density.glsl work; equivalent optimizations preserve the density field.
    - Reuse neighboring column descriptors within a sampled interval; retain height-dependent
      occupancy tests and native validity rules. Avoid neighbor lookups when they cannot affect erosion.
    - Skip noise when amplitude is zero and skip detailed work when cheap support proves zero density.
      Retain shared-face continuity, cubic bounds and world/wind anchoring for view and light rays.
    - Measure before changing procedural noise to a reusable noise texture; such a texture supplies
      detail only, not a complete density volume. A texture reproducing the current field requires
      same-field equivalence checks. An intentional noise-field replacement is a separate approximation
      choice: record statistical/output differences diagnostically and obtain user appearance review,
      without automated spectral-quality or fidelity thresholds. Both choices retain native support,
      connected faces, anchoring and the shared view/light density definition. Retain current noise
      if no measured benefit justifies replacement.
  ☑ Completion: specified atmospheric composition/OIT arithmetic passes correctness checks;
    density-preserving optimizations and phase hoisting pass same-algorithm equivalence checks.
    Intentional atmosphere/noise approximation changes have diagnostic comparisons and the stated
    review evidence rather than old-output equivalence gates. Attributed savings come from measurements.

Phase 7: Make acceleration optional and efficient for the new integration schedule
  Depends on Phase 6. Outcome: empty-space rejection helps without repeated dense-path overhead.
  ☑ Compare plain column traversal, cheap root rejection and the current hierarchy under identical
    new sample budgets; include construction and GPU barriers in total cost.
    - Consider cheap conservative density rejection and coarse start-distance rejection without
      assuming another prepass or a particular tree traversal is required.
  ☑ For retained hierarchical traversal, carry node/interval progress through the ray and reuse
    valid ancestor bounds instead of restarting root descent after every surviving column.
    - Traverse coherent node intervals front-to-back, ascend only when leaving their footprint and
      advance across missed regions without replaying every skipped native grid boundary twice.
    - Preserve conservative geometry, boundary ties, parallel axes, padding, thin positive density
      and forward progress. Define numerical tolerances independently of old repeated-addition clocks.
      Revisit the recorded baseline one-event secondary-counter discrepancies when validating
      numerical progress; isolate instrumentation effects from actual missed or duplicated support.
    - Preserve the new sample schedule across acceleration choices; skipped empty nodes must not
      change quadrature, primary jitter or secondary midpoint positions. Retain bounded direct-march
      fallback on the same lattice when summaries are unavailable; do not restore per-column budgets.
    - Keep certificates and generation rebuild rules for any retained summaries; never sample stale
      data. Stop allocating/building summaries when the selected policy has no active consumer.
  ☑ Select the production acceleration policy from matched measurements. Retain cheap rejection or
    direct marching where a tree does not pay for itself; do not require hierarchy use merely because
    it exists. Any DDA retained here locates support for the same scheduled points, not integration cells.
    - Avoid an unmeasured per-frame heuristic, CPU texture readback or settings polling. Record
      selection inputs, transitions, resource demand and any remaining workload regressions.
    - If the tree is retired, remove its unused production resources/program registration and update
      current architecture/catalog contracts; retain only useful independent validation or benchmarks.
  ☑ Completion: acceleration preserves the same scheduled evaluations and transport results, with
    tolerances limited to floating-point evaluation differences in equivalent implementations.
    These are correctness checks, not acceptable approximation-error limits; sample placement,
    conservative support and clipping contracts remain unchanged. Total costs support the selected
    policy and dense/inside-cloud regressions are resolved or open.

Phase 8: Reduce repeated cloud lighting and density work
  Depends on Phase 7. Outcome: measured shading savings at unchanged settings, with separately
    reviewed alternatives for approximation, resolution and sampling policy.
  ☑ Freeze the current candidate and establish representative remaining costs before implementation.
    - Record source/package identities, actual uploaded controls, trace/reconstruction dimensions,
      termination value and admitted light sources. Distinguish configured maxima, scheduled points,
      contributing samples and actual density/shadow evaluations; do not call a cap observed work.
    - Reuse opt-in measurements and existing GPU/program/resource abstractions. Measure construction,
      trace, temporal, resolve, CPU submission and independently timed total; keep uploads/readbacks
      outside trace timing and account for them separately in whole-renderer costs.
    - Include a representative native/full-view capture plus lean dense/inside and held-out night
      cases. Preserve the game-running instruction and disclose contention. Existing synthetic
      component results are prioritization evidence, not a full-view baseline or additive savings.
  ☑ Evaluate reusable cloud-internal shadow attenuation against bounded secondary marching.
    - Design a cloud-owned light-space attenuation representation derived from the same committed
      native map and placement, with explicit coverage, depth/units, filtering, source and generation
      validity. Include off-camera occluders and inside-cloud queries; scene depth must not clip it.
    - Compare generation, synchronization, lookup and total costs at identical view/light settings.
      Include low-angle lighting, moving weather, source motion, map boundaries and dense/sparse cases.
      State the representation's approximation and finite coverage; retain a valid bounded-march
      fallback when data is unavailable or outside coverage. Never silently reuse stale attenuation.
    - Review measured tradeoffs before selecting production behavior. If selected, use existing
      resource lifetimes, shader bindings, change-driven demand and same-frame publication; allocate
      only for active consumers and retire on disable/reload/world replacement or failed creation.
      Do not add a density volume, CPU map readback, external cast shadows or previous-frame publication.
  ☑ Remove unnecessary directional-light paths without assuming sun/moon exclusivity.
    - Compare one- and two-source shader specialization for actually admitted sources; handle solar,
      lunar, both, zero and twilight transitions in both PBR modes. Compile out an unused source where
      measurements justify the variants; do not confuse cheap rejected calls with actual shadow rays.
    - Retain the delivered negligible-source shortcut and its unoccluded contribution unless a
      measured replacement supersedes it. Keep eligibility outside the primary loop.
    - Evaluate the existing dominant-direct-light selection as a separate policy alternative, including
      environment accounting and twilight appearance. Do not silently discard a meaningful source or
      claim that a one-source policy is equivalent to shading both published sources.
  ☑ Reduce repeated detailed-density cost at unchanged detail controls.
    - Isolate noise arithmetic, neighbor support queries and descriptor/cache overhead in primary and
      secondary work. Hoist invariants and reuse only valid descriptors; verify equivalent optimizations
      against the same finite algorithm rather than adding approximation-quality assertions.
    - Evaluate footprint-/shadow-distance-dependent detail and cheaper reusable detail evaluation as
      explicit alternatives. Specify filtering, wind/periodic anchoring, connected-face behavior and
      source-admission bounds; distinguish arithmetic equivalence from a changed density approximation.
      Keep the native 2D control map and cubic envelope; review visual tradeoffs before policy changes.
  ☑ Reevaluate effective sampling, resolution and termination without changing the initial settings.
    - Diagnose why quarter-dimension tracing did not save time in the small contended experiment;
      verify actual pass extents, phase scheduling, work counts and total costs at representative size.
    - Report effective primary allocation versus path length and root-clipped executed ranges; compare
      budget-distance/max choices in this world's units. Do not infer actual work from 384 alone or
      increase counts to satisfy retired approximation tests.
    - Verify the fractional termination control preserves .005 default and any saved zero; quantify
      disabled versus enabled termination separately, including support and all OIT products. Do not
      migrate saved values or attribute a threshold change to an implementation-only speedup.
    - Keep lower-cost sampling/resolution/termination proposals separate from unchanged-setting
      improvements. Any production/default policy change requires reviewed evidence and user acceptance.
  ☑ Profile cloud-specific OIT arithmetic and payload bandwidth after the dominant lighting work.
    - Attribute per-step bucket/log/exp work and reconstruction traffic. Evaluate reusable invariant
      terms and narrower payload formats with explicit radiance/depth ranges and storage admission.
      Preserve six-target OIT order/arithmetic, support/spacing metadata and scene encoding exactly
      for equivalent changes; report any quantization or changed approximation separately for review.
    - Do not assume format conversion, shader specialization or a larger cache improves performance;
      retain only measured wins and report held-out regressions rather than hiding them in averages.
  ☑ Validate the selected changes with focused correctness checks and matched before/after timing.
    - Reuse existing fixtures; add only coverage for new contracts/failure paths, without raw GL,
      redundant matrices, approximation-quality gates, FPS assertions or mandatory noise/RMS limits.
    - Preserve uniform primary steps, quadratic shadow intervals, per-ray phase and root-only support
      clipping for retained marching paths. Do not rebuild a tree walk or change these correct policies
      merely to seek parity. Keep actual policy alternatives clearly identified in comparisons.
    - Record paired distributions, hardware/contention, hashes and raw results; distinguish synthetic
      shader savings from native whole-frame improvement. A lower sample count or fewer logical probes
      is not a measured speedup. Do not promise a target FPS from a different scene or renderer.
    - Run focused checks/canonical build through subagents; verify tested runtime/shader assets against
      published/package copies. Reconcile linked proposals/checklists and delivered architecture only
      after selection; leave unchanged contracts and separate reflected-cloud integration intact.
  ☑ Completion: selected changes have measured total benefit, focused correctness/package evidence,
    second review and independent audit. Alternative policies are selected or explicitly rejected/
    deferred with evidence; unresolved regressions and user-run appearance/full-view gates remain open.

Phase 9: Qualify the complete renderer and reconcile acceptance
  Depends on Phase 8. Outcome: the combined policy has reproducible quality and performance evidence.
  ☐ Freeze the candidate; run canonical build/shader, focused numerical/GPU, lifecycle and package
    checks through subagents. Verify production asset digests and exact output copies.
    - Cover Off/Simple/Volumetric, both PBR selections, pause, resize, reload, world replacement,
      source invalidation, current settings/defaults, reference selection and failed-resource recovery.
    - Keep density/bounds oracles independent; a new integrator must not validate only against itself.
  ☐ Measure baseline versus final renderer with matched scenes/settings and separate quality labels.
    - Report each increment's effects where attributable; distinguish fewer pixels/samples and
      changed approximation quality from same-output traversal/implementation improvements.
    - Include empty/sparse/dense clouds; ground/inside/above views; thin edges, low-angle solar/lunar,
      moving weather/light, odd maps and foreground/translucent/liquid overlap in both PBR modes.
    - Include the prior 512x512 regression matrix and representative full-view nonempty resolutions.
      Separate adversarial synthetic cases from native captures and live game evidence.
    - Record construction, trace, temporal, resolve, independently timed total, CPU submission,
      nominal storage, measured resident memory when available, hardware, hashes and raw samples.
      Account for native producer/uploads and shared depth separately in whole-frame assessment.
    - Report paired distributions and all regressions. Relative improvement is required; do not
      invent an absolute time target, hide regressions in an aggregate or equate probe counts with speed.
  ☐ Obtain user-run acceptance of the changed quality policy: cubic fluff, lighting, shadows within
    clouds, fog, thin support, disocclusion, motion and OIT overlap across relevant views/modes.
    Earlier appearance acceptance does not establish acceptance of changed sampling/resolution/fog.
  ☐ Update PBR.Clouds.md to describe only delivered behavior. Reconcile both source proposals,
    existing cloud checklists and the parent with implemented policy, remaining dependencies and
    receipts. Retire superseded requirements explicitly without rewriting prior measurement evidence.
  ☐ Completion: required correctness/package checks, second review, independent audit, measured
    relative benefit and user acceptance exist; unresolved performance or appearance gates stay open.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Whole-ray primary budgets, bounded retained secondary marches and qualified threshold
    termination replace the identified unbounded-by-quality per-column work multiplication.
    Any selected attenuation cache has separately bounded construction, coverage and lookup costs.
  ☐ Lower-resolution tracing, amortized atmosphere, repeated density/phase work and acceleration
    each have delivered behavior or an evidence-backed disposition against the stated objective.
  ☐ All six original performance findings, earlier density/phase findings and Phase 8 tasks map to final receipts;
    any unimplemented improvement is explicitly recorded, not silently treated as complete.
  ☐ Native ownership/weather, cubic density support, physical lighting, foreground clips, temporal
    lifecycle, existing OIT integration and both PBR modes satisfy inherited unaffected contracts.
  ☐ The parent remains unchecked while required live/performance gates or its separate reflected-
    cloud integration dependency remain outstanding. No headless result establishes live frame time.

Bounded quality policy (implementation contract)
  This section specifies the complete target behavior. Primary controls are implemented separately
    from later secondary, termination and reconstruction changes. Candidate defaults below are fixed
    starting inputs for comparisons, not a claim of validated appearance or a shipped default.
  Phase 8 may evaluate replacements for repeated self-lighting/detail work and separate quality-control
    choices. Until selection and reconciliation, the retained marching contracts below remain the
    baseline. A selected cached/filtered alternative must state its changed approximation explicitly;
    it cannot claim exact same-density/sample-local equivalence merely by preserving control values.

  Work accounting and units
    - Runtime retains one metre per world unit and 50-world-unit native columns. Extinction is
      map.r/(50*metresPerWorldUnit); path lengths enter transport in metres exactly once.
    - Geometry queries and OIT bookkeeping are separate from full density/source-lighting evaluations.
      Count both categories; a bounded shading budget does not imply constant acceleration overhead.
    - Report actual primary rays, secondary rays, primary/secondary density evaluations, native leaf
      fetches, hierarchy queries and skipped nodes separately where measured. An upper bound or CPU
      oracle estimate must not be presented as an observed GPU counter.

  Primary budget
    - Intersect the finite horizontal map domain and current main-view opaque/primary/liquid clips
      to obtain [tStart,tEnd]. A zero-length span performs no march. Let L=tEnd-tStart, including
      empty gaps. Use that same span across acceleration choices. Certified height bounds may reject
      an empty ray or skip proven-empty lattice positions; they do not redefine the sample count,
      spacing or origin. Do not add a support prepass to calculate occupied length or optical mass.
    - Set Nview=min(ViewRaySampleMax,max(ViewRaySampleMin,
      floor(ViewRaySampleMax*min(1,L/ViewBudgetDistance)))). Set ds=L/Nview. Sample positions are
      tStart+(i+jitter)*ds for i=0..Nview-1, with one blue-noise jitter in [0,1) shared along this ray.
      The history-free deterministic reference uses jitter=.5 on the same uniform distance lattice.
    - Current primary controls are ViewRaySampleMax=384, ViewRaySampleMin=1 and
      ViewBudgetDistance=50 world units. Earlier tuning receipts remain historical; retired
      approximation thresholds do not justify increasing these controls. Final cost and
      appearance selection remains separate measured/manual work.
      Supported maxima are 4..384; minimum is 1..min(16,max); distance is 50..10000 world units.
      Nonfinite distance restores 50; sanitize integral controls before conversion/upload.
    - At each position evaluate cheap native validity/support first, then the existing detailed
      density if potentially occupied. Positive floating-point weight (transmission*alpha) evaluates
      incident lighting at that same position and starts at most one shadow ray per nonzero source.
      Exactly zero weight skips lighting and centroid accumulation, retaining every density,
      support, transmission and native-bin update; it does not change the termination setting.
      Do not reuse a detail factor or source from another interval, or redistribute unused samples.
    - Use sigma(sample)*dsMetres for optical depth and the existing analytic constant-segment
      scattering integral. Apply native depth-bin weights at each integration step; preserve all
      six OIT outputs without flattening the whole cloud to a representative transparency depth.
    - Native columns define a discontinuous density field. Thin features may fall between finite
      samples; quantify pointwise, temporal and silhouette errors against independent references.
      Retain exact uniform-density optical-depth fixtures. Exact integration of arbitrary columns
      belongs to the analytic oracle, not an additional production column-integration pass.

  Secondary budget and distance
    - Default reduced tracing builds a 256x256 same-frame light-space attenuation map with 64
      detailed-density quadrature samples per map ray for the stronger published source. Nearest
      lookup approximates extinction as uniform between first/last occupied distances, clipped to
      the query's ShadowDistance. This supersedes sample-local marching only for that cached source;
      both meaningful lights remain represented. Generation includes off-camera density and uses
      certified root bounds, matching map/placement/lighting and completed write/sample barriers.
      Unavailable, invalid or uncovered lookup falls back to the bounded schedule below. No previous-
      frame attenuation is retained. The full-resolution reference always retains marching.
    - Initial candidate LightRaySampleMax=10, supported 2..64, and ShadowDistance=1500 world units,
      supported 50..10000. Nonfinite distance restores 1500. Clip to the finite map; never use main-
      view depth or visibility to reject off-camera shadow density.
    - Anchor the schedule at the shading point. For D=ShadowDistance and N=LightRaySampleMax,
      use interval boundaries b_i=D*(i/N)^2, i=0..N, positions s_i=(b_i+b_(i+1))/2 and lengths
      d_i=b_(i+1)-b_i. Accumulate tau+=sigma(origin+direction*s_i)*d_i*metresPerWorldUnit.
      The sample is the arithmetic midpoint of quadratic boundaries, not D*((i+.5)/N)^2.
    - Use deterministic midpoint positions for solar and lunar shadows; no PMJ, blue-noise jitter,
      optical-depth weighting or per-column budget reset enters the shadow schedule. Reject proven
      zero density before detailed evaluation, but leave all remaining scheduled positions unchanged.
    - Positions outside the finite supported domain contribute zero. Stop when the ray has exited
      that domain with no possible reentry; do not rescale D or redistribute positions after such
      an exit. Respect the complete quadratic schedule for rays that remain inside supported bounds.
      An existing certified vertical bound may similarly prove remaining samples empty.
    - The same density definition is evaluated independently at every scheduled primary/shadow
      position. No replacement vertical profile, density volume or invented border extension is added.
    - Before shadow integration, bound a directional source by illuminance*(phase+octaveContribution/
      (4*pi)). If every channel is strictly below 1e-5 of that environment channel, retain this
      unoccluded response without a shadow march. The omitted attenuation is bounded by 0.001%
      of the environment per channel; zero background channels prevent the shortcut. Uncached night
      lighting retains the complete shadow schedule. Sample controls and optical products stay fixed.
    - At selected default budgets after bounded secondary integration, a primary ray has at most
      768 secondary rays and 7680 secondary full-detail evaluations with both sources active, plus
      at most 384 primary evaluations; these are policy
      upper bounds, not baseline observations. Report actual early exits and skipped zero sources.
    - Optional diagnostics compare 1500,3000 and complete supported-domain shadow distances with
      other settings fixed. Report distance bias separately from sampling bias; neither is an
      automated quality gate or a requirement to increase sample count/distance.

  Atmospheric composition
    - Integrate physical incident cloud radiance L=sum(w_i*S_i), opacity measure W=sum(w_i) and
      distance moment M=sum(w_i*t_i), with w_i=transmission-before-step*alpha_i. Only admitted,
      contributing finite-budget samples enter these sums; include the terminating sample.
    - For W>0 use d=M/W and R=W*CloudTransport(L/W,direction*d). Air/local-fog in-scattering
      is thereby multiplied by cloud opacity, never added unweighted. W=0 leaves neutral output.
      Apply completed aerial decode or the existing underwater/local-medium branch once; retain
      physical units, current publication admission, medium/history certificates and RGB encoding.
    - Own this operation in primary trace/direct transport before physical history. Resolve and
      temporal reconstruction never reevaluate the medium. Reload/reset invalidates prior physical
      history. Routine lighting/fog updates retain neighborhood-bounded history; changing between
      aerial and underwater transport invalidates the previous medium mode.
    - The compact payload already shares one radiance across native buckets; use one opacity mean
      without adding bucket-specific radiance storage. Each primary step still updates its actual
      native bucket transmission and support depth. This does not assign all cloud opacity to d.
      Distance-varying medium and source-distance correlations can differ from per-sample transport;
      record those differences diagnostically and retain user-run appearance qualification.
    - Hoist fixed directional phase for each nonzero light before the primary loop, retaining
      independent per-point shadow integration and its zero-source rejection. Cache native column
      descriptors only across consecutive samples in that column; retest neighbors at every height.
      Noise and native geometry remain unchanged. Do not infer individual timing savings from
      source operation counts or from combined renderer measurements.
  Acceleration policy
    - Reduced tracing uses certified root-height rejection, with direct native support as fallback.
      The root can reject a whole primary ray, but its clipped vertical interval never replaces
      the horizontal primary span used for counts, spacing or blue-noise jitter. Shadow roots
      can skip proven-empty parts of the fixed quadratic schedule without rescaling its distance.
      Primary root entry/exit distances bound indices on the unchanged lattice; keep outward
      floor/ceil guard indices rather than querying clear air before/after that supported range.
      Original indices still define sample positions, distance weights and native OIT depths.
    - Root intervals expand outward by eight binary32 epsilons times the maximum of one, absolute
      root heights and abs(origin.y)+abs(direction.y)*spanEnd. Nearly horizontal rays compare both
      finite-span endpoint heights instead of assuming constant height. Nonfinite bounds retain
      native support checks. Numerical uncertainty admits work rather than discarding support.
    - Keep the existing exact-generation GPU reduction/certificate and direct fallback. Lower
      mips are construction intermediates consumed by the root reduction. No primary/secondary
      DDA or root-to-leaf tree walk remains in production; surviving samples query their native cells.
      Cached scheduled-point hierarchy rejection remains an explicit measurement alternative.
    - Selection is fixed for the reduced consumer, not a per-frame weather/readback/settings
      heuristic. Off, Simple and the history-free reference require no height-summary allocation.
      Missing/failed/stale summaries use the identical scheduled native queries; native ownership,
      resource retirement and successful-commit-only history rotation remain unchanged.

  Sampling and temporal identity
    - Use spatial blue-noise primary-ray offsets with an eight-frame temporal index advancing every
      rendered frame, independent of reconstruction phase. Use the traced full-view coordinate;
      a hash or PMJ point sequence alone is not a spatial blue-noise mask. Use existing resource
      ownership/binding APIs for any required noise asset; do not import an external engine runtime.
    - Separate jitter evolution from spatial reconstruction scheduling. Quarter-dimension trace to
      half-dimension reconstruction has a 2x2/four-frame phase cycle in reconstruction coordinates.
      Full-view upsampling does not turn that into sixteen phases. The retained Half compatibility
      mode may keep its existing phase policy until its own reconstruction adaptation is verified.
    - Shadow midpoints have no temporal jitter. The deterministic full-view reference uses primary
      midpoint steps and the same quadratic shadow midpoints. Release the cloud PMJ resource/bindings
      when no retained legacy comparison consumer uses them; leave unrelated consumers untouched.
    - Reject history on any change to sample budgets/distances, lattice/jitter policy, termination,
      trace/reconstruction layout or atmosphere application policy, as well as existing lifecycle,
      camera, aerial/underwater transport-mode and calibration incompatibilities. Routine changes
      to completed lighting, sun/moon directions, atmospheric response and fog retain accumulation.
    - Reproject camera-relative cloud points with projection times the rotation-only previous view.
      Native eye translation must not enter that matrix: the placement delta supplies translation.
      Leave the shared scene-camera transform convention unchanged.
    - Preserve primary spacing in the fourth bucket-payload channel as support metadata, not an
      optical factor. Include the sum of current/previous spacings in support midpoint tolerance
      and twice that sum in thickness tolerance. Retain foreground depth and displacement rejection.
      Quarter converts contributing spacings to destination-ray units and retains their maximum.
      Store the current spacing without temporal averaging. Bound optical history by the compatible
      current neighborhood instead of rejecting stochastic attenuation changes with a fixed delta.

  Termination policy
    - Candidate view transmittance threshold=.005; supported range [0,.005], with zero disabling this
      optimization. It controls the primary exit directly; its value alone does not qualify OIT quality.
    - Break the primary march after the current integration step if overall transmittance is below
      the threshold. Do not add a remaining-support prepass, a second attenuation/bin march or a
      residual-error subsystem. Retain ordinary completed-lighting admission and finite storage bounds.
    - Verify configured termination at the correct sample boundary and retain finite arithmetic.
      Record differences in revealage, bins, accumulators, glow and metadata without automated
      approximation-quality limits. Threshold selection uses measured cost and user appearance review.
    - Shadow rays initially evaluate the bounded quadratic schedule until finite-domain exit, with
      no optical-depth saturation shortcut. Keep tau finite and handle zero octave occlusion without
      zero*infinity. Additional shortcuts are optional future work requiring separate evidence.

  Quality selections and clean-break settings
    - Introduce separately named ViewRaySampleMax, ViewRaySampleMin,
      ViewBudgetDistance, LightRaySampleMax, ShadowDistance, StopTransmittance and TraceScale fields
      through the existing configuration owner. Keep detail fields and FullResolutionReference.
    - TraceScale is an enum with Half and Quarter; initial compatibility selection is Half. Quarter
      becomes the normal selection only after correctness, performance and user appearance review.
      FullResolutionReference overrides trace scale, disables temporal history and uses midpoint
      primary sampling and quadratic shadow midpoints; it is not the independent high-budget oracle.
    - User direction requires a clean break: no migration, compatibility converter, schema-version
      metadata or future-version preservation subsystem. Missing current fields use current defaults;
      obsolete fields are ignored, never converted into new counts. Preserve unrelated settings through
      the existing owner and save normally, not per frame. Retire replaced source-level fields too.
    - Validate absent Clouds, empty Clouds, obsolete ViewSamples, explicit new budgets, invalid bounds,
      nonfinite distances/thresholds and partial current-setting updates. Primary defaults are
      384/2/50; light controls are 10/1500 and StopTransmittance is .005. Layout controls receive
      their stated defaults when implemented.

  Correctness verification and optional approximation diagnostics
    - Keep independent scalar/native-box oracles for the specified sample lattice, shared density,
      finite domain, units and OIT/encoding formulas. Assert equivalent implementations on the same
      scheduled positions, with tolerances appropriate to floating-point arithmetic.
    - Keep exact uniform-density Beer-Lambert checks only when density is uniform over the complete
      sampled span and early termination is disabled or does not trigger. Keep settings/budget bounds,
      deterministic replay, source combinations, octave endpoints, finite outputs, depth clipping,
      resource lifetimes and history-rejection correctness.
    - Native support is discontinuous. Production versus exact/high-budget integration, reference
      doubling, approximation changes, image envelopes, temporal means and filter RMS/noise comparisons
      are optional measurements without approximation-quality acceptance assertions. They do not block
      implementation completion or mandate increased sample counts, tracing distances or resolution.
    - Diagnostic references must state their own approximation/stability limitations. Report physical
      RGB and OIT differences with explicit normalization and coverage; a normalized radiometric error
      is not a perceptual error. No mandatory reference-convergence or approximation-error threshold
      applies, including the former .01/.02/.021/.045 limits or frozen-baseline RMS requirements.
    - Finite attenuation bounds and admitted foreground clipping remain correctness requirements for
      every frame. Contributing metadata describes the steps that actually contributed, clipped to
      the admitted span; it need not match exact unsampled support. Authored temporal arithmetic and
      stale-history rejection remain tested independently of denoising effectiveness.
    - Approximation assertions and unused dense-reference machinery are removed in Phase 3.1.
      Current correctness verification needs no approximation-test exclusions. Neither a passing
      correctness suite nor diagnostics establish appearance acceptance or measured performance.

  Requirement reconciliation
    - Primary per-column multiplication -> Phase 2; secondary multiplication/distance -> Phase 3;
      strict/late termination -> Phase 4; screen-position count -> Phase 5; repeated atmosphere ->
      Phase 6; repeated tree descent/clock replay -> Phase 7; repeated density/phase work -> Phase 6.
    - Proposal half-dimension default is the legacy/current contract; Phase 5 may replace it only
      after the declared lower-resolution gates. Preserved per-column sample counts, PMJ identity and
      exact arbitrary-column attenuation govern legacy/reference comparisons. The future direct march
      replaces those constraints with fixed distance steps, primary blue noise and shadow midpoints.
    - With termination inactive, complete-span uniform-density attenuation remains exact within numeric
      tolerance. Discontinuous/detailed
      quadrature differences are diagnostic; acceleration equivalence is measured with the new lattice held
      fixed. No requirement to use a tree remains if measured cost favors cheap rejection or direct marching.
    - PBR.Clouds.md remains current-state architecture until runtime changes are verified. Preserve
      atmospheric publication, native producer/lifetimes, finite density bounds, six-target OIT,
      separate scene/material/particle/liquid depth and the unresolved reflection dependency.

Phase 1 source reinspection and task traceability
  Source reinspection -> CloudVolumeSettings.cs / CloudRenderingSettings.cs / CloudVolumeParameters.cs
    under VanillaGraphicsExpanded/PBR/Clouds -> current defaults are 4 view and 1 light subdivision
    per occupied column, detail on, amplitude .15, edge width 2, noise scale .08, octave .5/.5.
    Parameters validate width 1..200 and use the committed native placement; runtime units are one
    metre per world unit. Source identities belong to the frozen baseline receipt, not this prose.
  Source reinspection -> shaders/includes/cloud_volume_transport.glsl / cloud_volume_light.glsl /
    cloud_volume_density.glsl / cloud_volume_hierarchy.glsl -> current view/light loops allow 402
    column iterations; view/detail quality is allocated per occupied column, secondary distance is
    1e7 before finite map clipping, view exit is 1e-6 after the column. Hierarchy seeks restart at
    root and replay boundary additions. Every detailed evaluation queries neighbors and value noise.
    The same density definition is used by view/light; native r is extinction, not alpha.
  Source reinspection -> CloudVolumeReconstruction.cs / CloudVolumeStorage.cs /
    CloudTemporalHistory.cs and cloud_volume_trace/temporal/resolve shaders -> current trace and
    temporal passes are ceil-half view dimensions; resolve is full view and texture-only. Nine
    RGBA32F array layers rotate in three disjoint blocks. History uses current lifecycle, settings,
    frame, placement, projection, medium and completed-lighting identity; phase is a 2x2 cycle.
  Source reinspection -> cloud_volume_transport.glsl / atmosphere_aerial.glsl /
    CloudVolumeLighting.cs / PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md -> current contributing samples apply
    aerial or local underwater transport before integration, using completed physical illumination
    and once-only scene encoding. Cloud source bounds join frame admission; exposure changes do
    not alter attenuation/history. A representative-distance approximation is future work.
  OIT requirements -> PBR.VolumetricClouds.Proposal.md, Scene integration; PBR.Clouds.md, Density,
    lighting and scene composition; PBR/Liquids/LiquidPipelineStates.cs and shaders/includes/oit.glsl
    -> retain Reveal/Reveal/Glow/Add/Add/Add outputs. Cloud bins use distributed adjusted depth;
    overall transmission alone does not prove all bin/accumulation channels have converged.
  Baseline task -> CloudHierarchyPerformanceTests / CloudPerformanceScene / CloudPerformanceTimer;
    PBR.CloudHierarchy.Proposal.md, Acceptance and Ownership; inherited checklist measurement receipts
    -> same-candidate DDA/hierarchy pairs, fresh generation reduction, both PBR selections, exact
    selected history/OIT checks, stable allocations, separate stages/CPU/total and raw observations.
    Historical phase6-512-result-summary.json establishes comparison methodology, not current speed.
  Quality/settings task -> Bounded quality policy above; CloudRenderingSettings.cs and
    CloudTemporalHistoryTests.cs -> explicit names/limits, clean-break defaults, fixed-distance
    sample allocation, independent oracles and frozen error limits. Future production controls and
    transport behavior remain subject to the later implementation tasks and their tests.
  Reconciliation task -> both cloud proposals and their checklists, PBR.BaselineShading.todo cloud
    requirements, PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md and Rendering.Enablement.md -> future sampling/layout
    policy replaces only the identified quality constraints; preserve native/atmosphere ownership,
    finite geometry, change-driven demand, current scene depth clips and existing OIT. Parent cloud
    completion, reflection integration and changed-renderer appearance remain separate open gates.

